WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.257
A common mistake is skipping analysis.

00:00:02.257 --> 00:00:07.291
When teams assume they already know the problem, they often build the wrong course.

00:00:07.291 --> 00:00:10.167
The result may explain information learners already understand,

00:00:10.167 --> 00:00:11.965
ignore the real performance barrier,

00:00:11.965 --> 00:00:16.998
or force eLearning into a problem better solved by a job aid, process change,

00:00:16.998 --> 00:00:18.436
coaching, or clearer communication.

00:00:18.436 --> 00:00:20.594
Another mistake is using vague objectives.

00:00:20.594 --> 00:00:25.627
Objectives such as know, understand, or be aware can be useful as informal language,

00:00:25.627 --> 00:00:28.144
but they rarely guide design by themselves.

00:00:28.144 --> 00:00:32.818
If the objective does not identify what learners should do with the knowledge,

00:00:32.818 --> 00:00:36.773
the course may drift into content coverage rather than performance support.

00:00:36.773 --> 00:00:39.649
Copying source documents into slides is also common.

00:00:39.649 --> 00:00:43.244
Source material is valuable, but it is not automatically instruction.

00:00:43.244 --> 00:00:47.199
Policies, manuals, and procedure documents often need translation into examples, decisions,

00:00:47.199 --> 00:00:48.637
scenarios, summaries, and practice.

00:00:48.637 --> 00:00:51.873
The course should help learners use the source material,

00:00:51.873 --> 00:00:55.109
not simply reproduce it in a less convenient format.

00:00:55.109 --> 00:00:58.704
Interactions and media can become mistakes when added without purpose.

00:00:58.704 --> 00:01:02.659
A click-to-reveal interaction that hides ordinary text may slow learners down.

00:01:02.659 --> 00:01:06.974
A video that could have been a simple diagram may waste time.

00:01:06.974 --> 00:01:10.569
A quiz that asks trivia may not measure the objective.

00:01:10.569 --> 00:01:14.165
Every course element should have an instructional reason to exist.

00:01:14.165 --> 00:01:14.815
Finally,

00:01:14.815 --> 00:01:20.567
teams sometimes treat LMS upload as the end of implementation and completion as the only evaluation.

00:01:20.567 --> 00:01:24.163
Real implementation includes launch testing, communication, access, support, and tracking.

00:01:24.163 --> 00:01:27.758
Real evaluation asks whether the course improved the intended outcome.

00:01:27.758 --> 00:01:33.220
ADDIE becomes bureaucracy only when teams follow the labels without using them to make better decisions.
